[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"DIP: Dependency Injection Containers and Wiring Architecture"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) is the final pillar of the SOLID principles. It states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions. Furthermore, abstractions should not depend on details. Dependency Injection (DI) and DI Containers are the architectural mechanisms used to implement DIP effectively in modern software systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Wiring Problem"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a tightly coupled application, high-level business logic directly instantiates low-level infrastructure concerns, such as database repositories or HTTP clients, using the 'new' keyword. This creates rigid codebases that are difficult to test and maintain."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class OrderProcessor {
    private SqlDatabase database;

    public OrderProcessor() {
        this.database = new SqlDatabase(); // Tightly coupled violation
    }

    public void process(Order order) {
        database.save(order);
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying DIP Through Abstraction"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"To decouple the classes, we introduce an interface (the abstraction). The high-level module now depends only on the interface, while the low-level module implements it."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"interface Database {
    void save(Order order);
}

class SqlDatabase implements Database {
    public void save(Order order) {
        // Database logic
    }
}

class OrderProcessor {
    private Database database;

    public OrderProcessor(Database database) {
        this.database = database; // Dependency Injection via constructor
    }

    public void process(Order order) {
        database.save(order);
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of Dependency Injection Containers"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"While constructor injection solves the coupling problem, manual wiring at the composition root of a large application becomes extremely tedious. A Dependency Injection Container (or IoC Container) is a framework tool that automates the instantiation and wiring of object graphs."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Registration: Mapping interfaces to concrete implementations within the container registry."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Resolution: Inspecting constructor signatures and automatically resolving nested dependencies recursively."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lifecycle Management: Controlling whether instances are Singletons, Transient, or Scoped per request."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Architectural Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Establish a single Composition Root at the absolute entry point of your application where the container is configured."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid Service Locator anti-pattern; classes should not be aware of or pull from the container directly."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep domain and business logic layers free from third-party DI container annotations or dependencies."}]
